   180	   >   check on its own clock before signing; the issuing account able to enqueue but not to invoke the key, change
   181	   >   the policy or clock, or obtain key-use credentials; and the key dedicated to V1 type-0x00 documents. It also
   182	   >   preserves every property in the frozen custody record (`270ca49:docs/V1-PROFILE.md:1017-1040`) except the
   183	   >   identity of the implementation, its 2026-10-14 deadline evidence and its prototype paths, which this option
   184	   >   replaces;
   185	   > - (ii) the external custody record names the deployed implementation, its image digest and its configuration;
   186	   > - (iii) deployed evidence is earned on the new bytes. That means: a deployed positive signature that verifies
   187	   >   offline; deployed refusals for a malformed frame, a wrong tag, a wrong alg, a wrong fingerprint and a declared
   188	   >   time outside ε; a review of every principal that can cause a signature or administer the signer
   189	   >   (`service/issuer/DEPLOY.md` step 8); and verified retirement of every other signing path (step 7);
   190	   > - (iv) all of it identifies the tested image and configuration.
   191	   >
   192	   > This keeps FREEZE-RECORD.md:62, A9.2 and A9.6's custody and policy requirements. It overrides the code-identity
   193	   > reading of "the selected, deadline-qualified custody implementation" (profile :1024-1025) and of A9.6's "final
   194	   > custody implementation" (A9:389-390). Cost: it is a retrospective relaxation, made after the deadline and after
   195	   > the candidate code existed. It is disclosed as such in the header.
   196	   >
   197	   > **Option 2: the code-identity reading is kept (A9.6 fallback).** No port can be deadline-qualified, and the
   198	   > prototype cannot run production, so the hybrid's custody condition cannot be met. A9.6's own consequence
   199	   > applies: "otherwise the registered fallback is taken" (A9:391; cut order A9:211-212). That is classical P-256
   200	   > in both slots (A9:372-374), with slot 2 still under A9.6 custody through a ported service signer. The fallback
   201	   > has no 21 Oct custody deadline. This keeps A9.6, A9.2 and FREEZE-RECORD.md:62 literally. Cost: it drops the PQ
   202	   > slot. A9:346-350 records PQ as the author's stated preference, not a claim requirement, and the first item cut.
   203	   > It changes the frozen catalog's `alg` (`formal/freeze-v1/objects/catalog.json`; FREEZE-RECORD.md:16, :20), so
   204	   > the freeze must be amended or redone before issuing. The 2026-10-16 hybrid registration becomes moot.
   205	   >
   206	   > The author's ruling: ______ (pending).
   207	
   208	   **Owner recommendation (a recommendation, not a decision): Option 1.** It keeps every custody and policy
   209	   requirement and re-earns all evidence on the bytes that run. Option 2 keeps the deadline text literally but
   210	   gives up the author's stated preference and reopens the freeze. Until the author rules, issuance does not start
   211	   (§A11.7, `service/issuer/DEPLOY.md`).
   212	3. **The spike keys (`formal/p8/HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT.md` §11 item 6, :524-526).** The spike-key sentence (:525-526)
   213	   is **unchanged**: "Both spike keys are deleted after the re-capture verifies, once the review clears." The
   214	   item's first sentence (:524), "the issuer always uses EXTERNAL_MU for the kms slot", is changed by §A11.3 row 12
   215	   (K-R). The owner's deployment draft (2026-10-27) reused the spike keys as production keys. That contradicted
   216	   this commitment, and the draft is withdrawn. Production uses new keys (`service/issuer/DEPLOY.md`), recorded in
   217	   the external custody record before use.
   218	4. **A dangling cross-reference (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-10.md:675`).** Row 25 does not apply to V1 (E9 row 2,
   219	   2026-10-23; §A11.3 row 8). This is a CLARIFY: the fallback cites row 15 only (§A11.3 row 9).
   220	5. **K-R's enforcement (`formal/p8/HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT.md:61-64`, `:148-174`, `:524`, `:533`;
   221	   `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:557-559`): amended in wording, the assumption unchanged.** (K-R) itself stands: the
   222	   ML-DSA-65 key signs only V1 tagged strings, in pure mode with the empty context, under EXTERNAL_MU, for no other
   223	   protocol. What changes is who enforces it. The supplement and ENCODING-V1 §5.2 name the issuer as the computer
   224	   of `μ` and the issuer role as the only `kms:Sign` principal. In V1 as designed (deployment pending), a
   225	   dedicated policy signer validates the complete frame (tag, alg, key fingerprint, ε for attestation frames only,
   226	   `sign_by`, the signing stop) *before* computing `μ`. Manifests carry no declared time, so ε does not apply to
   227	   possession signing (HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT:495; `service/signer-kms/tessera_sign_kms/signer.py:180-184`). The
   228	   signer's role is the only `kms:Sign` principal (`service/signer-kms/`; key policy in
   229	   `service/signer-kms/infra/main.tf`). This narrows what a captured issuer role alone can get signed. It does not
   230	   change the supplement's stated limit: "whoever may call `kms:Sign` on the key can sign any message by computing
   231	   `μ`" (:171-172). An administrator of the account can still grant that (`PutKeyPolicy`), which is C3.
   232	
   233	## A11.6 Clarifications
   234	
   235	These are readings, not changes. Each narrows or clarifies only and adds no claim.
   236	
   237	1. **ENCODING-V1 §5.2 rule O *Reasons* (c021). Narrowing.** "ENCODING-V1 §5.2 rule O *Reasons*
   238	   (`formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:538-544`) record the 2026-10-12 GnuPG observation that motivated the legacy-header
   239	   form. Rule O itself is unchanged. Under the pair id, the gpg slot's packet is assembled by the slot-2 policy
   240	   signer (profile §8 custody record, `270ca49:docs/V1-PROFILE.md:1033-1036`), and it is a Tessera-assembled packet
   241	   held to the same form as :541 requires. :541's 'KMS slot's packet' describes the classical id only, because
   242	   under the pair id the kms slot emits rule R and has no packet."
   243	2. **W1 and receipt identity (c033). Clarifying.** "Profile §6 W1 (`270ca49:docs/V1-PROFILE.md:850-851`) 'is not an
   244	   identifier' has the sense of its cited source ENC §3.5 (`formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:400-402`): no semantic,
   245	   standing or attempt identifier, and no identifier of the statement, is derived from signature bytes. §9's 'A
   246	   receipt's identity is SHA256(A_sig)' (:1183-1187) is an exact-bytes identity for deduplicating and referring to
   247	   one receipt's claim-bearing bytes. Two receipts of one statement with different valid signatures are different
   248	   receipts. Neither text widens the other."
   249	3. **"Both OpenPGP adapters" (c056). Narrowing.** "Profile §6 issuer duties 'Both OpenPGP adapters'
   250	   (`270ca49:docs/V1-PROFILE.md:386-387`) and 'both OpenPGP adapters and the repository signer' (:392-393) mean
   251	   every OpenPGP signer in use. Under the pair id that is the gpg slot's policy signer (and the repository signer).
   252	   The kms slot emits rule R, to which the rule-O policy does not apply. Both apply under the classical id."
   253	
   254	**Not confirmed: c145.** It read profile §14.2 item 1's "YubiKey 2 is not in hand" (`270ca49:docs/V1-PROFILE.md:1396-1397`)
   255	as naming YubiKey 2 the release key. No text does. The runbook says the release key is "Not YubiKey 2"
   256	(`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md:125`). Nothing is needed.
   257	
   258	## A11.7 Owner-document repairs (not registered text)
   259	
   260	These are owner documents. The owner makes each repair outside this amendment. They are listed so the record shows
   261	where each one belongs.
   262	
   263	- **c016.** `docs/V1-SERVICE-RESOLUTIONS.md:23` (S4): the 24-hour terminal FAILED conflicts with A2's
   264	  `declared + δ + S` expiry (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:260-264`, 96 h at A9.2's values) and A9:422. S4 keeps
   265	  "usually complete within 3 hours" as a non-guaranteed figure. A request ends only by the registered lifecycle:
   266	  COMPLETE, or REFUSED when its N attempts are exhausted (A2.3). The issuer had no attempts, no latch and no
   267	  refusal, so this is more than an edit. The owner's design is `docs/decisions/2026-10-29-issuance-lifecycle.md`,
   268	  reviewed in `docs/reviews/issuance-lifecycle/`. It covers attempts, three-conjunct eligibility, a latch that
   269	  expiry can override only by re-observing the chain evidence, and an explicit REFUSED. Matching edits: CL-03 items
   270	  1 and 2 (`docs/V1-CLARIFICATIONS.md`), the clock-tolerance decision, `service/issuer/DESIGN.md`, and the issuer
   271	  code and tests (inventory in the design). A shorter hard bound needs a signed amendment (A2.1 `:139-142`).
   272	- **c036.** `service/issuer/DEPLOY.md:55-60`: steps 6–7 depend on the §A11.5 item 2 ruling. Until it, a line says
   273	  issuance cannot start. No prototype fallback is added. Under Option 2, steps 5–7 and the key section are
   274	  rewritten for the classical fallback.
   275	- **c093.** `formal/PROPERTIES.md:167-168`: add S, and record that for `tessera-v1-strict` all five parameters were
   276	  ratified by the author's signature on A9 (A9:223-225). The Band 0 exit item stays open outside V1 (§A11.3 row 4).
   277	- **c136.** `docs/V1-SERVICE-RESOLUTIONS.md:26` (S7): replace "(H3)" with a cost derived from H3's three
   278	  measurements, stated with its derivation. Per-receipt cost is not an H3 number (`phase-0-prereg.md:232-248`).
